data/raw/
models/
# `results/` alone excludes the directory, and git never descends into an
# excluded directory, so no negation beneath it can rescue a file. `results/*`
# excludes the contents instead, which lets the two negations below work.
# Verified on git 2.50.1: under `results/` the negations stage nothing.
results/*
!results/corpus/
!results/ceiling_study/
# The Campaign 3 analysis outputs. Every figure in
# docs/campaign3-audit-mechanism.md was hand-transcribed from stdout because
# these scripts default to --out=None and results/* excluded anything they
# wrote. A spending decision resting on numbers with no machine-readable
# counterpart cannot be diffed, and a re-run six months later against a changed
# corpus would be indistinguishable from a documentation error.
#
# Allowlisted to JSON only, for the same reason results/phase1b/ is: whatever
# an analysis script grows into later defaults to excluded.
!results/analysis/
results/analysis/*
!results/analysis/*.json
# The LLM pre-pass is a proxy, not a ceiling. The runbook is explicit that "a
# proxy labelled as a ceiling is the same category of error as the withdrawn
# accuracy figure", and four files with LLM_PROXY in the name sitting beside the
# real 250-item human study invite exactly that misreading. Committing them is a
# decision to take deliberately, with a header saying what they are.
results/ceiling_study/*LLM_PROXY*
results/ceiling_study/answers_llm_proxy.json
# `results/*` above also excluded results/phase1b, which is where the LOFO
# orchestrator writes and where runpod_parallel's `collect` rsyncs a fleet's
# output. Forty-five fold results were tracked before that rule landed, so the
# breakage was invisible: `git status` stayed clean while every NEW fold result
# a run produced was silently unstageable. A run on a rented fleet would have
# reached the end with its evidence on disk and nothing to push.
#
# Allowlist rather than denylist. The directory holds 2.2 GB of optimizer state
# and safetensors against a few MB of JSON, so naming what to keep is the safe
# direction: a new binary artifact type defaults to excluded instead of landing
# in git.
!results/phase1b/
results/phase1b/**
!results/phase1b/**/
!results/phase1b/**/*.json
!results/phase1b/**/*.md
# Weights last, so these win over the *.json line above. A saved model's
# tokenizer.json and config.json tracked without the tensors they describe read
# like a checkpoint that is present when it is not, and the tokenizers alone
# account for 35 of the 39 MB this section would otherwise expose.
results/phase1b/**/checkpoint-*/
results/phase1b/**/model/
# results/review is PHASE3_REVIEW_OUTPUT_DIR, and it carried the same defect in
# a quieter form: four tracked files inside an ignored directory, so a re-export
# under a new filename would have vanished while the existing four kept showing
# as modified. Everything it holds is JSON and markdown, so the whole directory
# is re-included rather than filtered.
!results/review/
# results/bridge holds bridge_report.json, the Phase 2B record of which bridge
# candidates an expert accepted. `tract bridge` writes it and nothing was
# reading it back out of git, because git could not see it.
!results/bridge/
# results/phase1c is excluded apart from one carve-out. crosswalk.db is covered
# by *.db, deployment_model/ is weights, and similarities/ is .npz arrays, so
# the directory defaults to excluded. calibration/ is the exception: ece_gate.json
# is a gate verdict, and a gate whose result cannot be committed is a gate
# nobody can check.
!results/phase1c/
results/phase1c/*
!results/phase1c/calibration/
*.db
__pycache__/
.env
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
*.pyc
.ruff_cache/
scripts/phase0/.pod_ids
scripts/phase0/.pod_state.json
scripts/phase1b/.pod_state.json
scripts/phase1b/.runpod_known_hosts
scripts/phase0/.runpod_known_hosts
scripts/phase0/.pod_state_0r.json
.claude/
wandb/
docs/session-prompts.md
hub_proposals/
.worktrees/
notebooks/.ipynb_checkpoints/
# The rule above only covered checkpoints under notebooks/, but the narrative
# notebook lives at the repository root and Jupyter writes its checkpoints
# beside it. That directory reached 8.1 MB of stale copies carrying the same
# outputs as the tracked notebook, which is a second, unscanned path for any
# text the licensed-text gate is meant to keep out of the tree.
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.DS_Store
# Specs and plans are the design record for a research project whose deliverable
# is reproducibility, and a criterion that lives in an untracked file can be
# edited mid-run with no diff. Scratch under docs/superpowers/ stays ignored;
# these two directories do not. Both are covered by the tree-wide licensed-text
# gate, which scans every tracked .md, and both measure zero licensed n-grams.
docs/superpowers/*
!docs/superpowers/plans/
!docs/superpowers/specs/
# This one plan quotes two ISO 27001 Annex A statements verbatim. The tree-wide
# gate caught it when the directory was first staged, which is the gate working.
# It stays ignored rather than being redacted, because editing a superseded plan
# to make it committable rewrites the record of what was actually planned.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-15-parser-contract-and-iso.md
docs/phase0-results.md
venv/
# Licensed framework text. One line per member of
# tract.config.RESTRICTED_FRAMEWORK_IDS, and
# tests/test_licensed_text_not_tracked.py fails if a member is missing a line.
# These publishers reserve reproduction and this repository is CC0, which is an
# affirmative grant that the publisher holds the rights and waives them.
# Committing the prose here would assert rights we do not hold, and `git push`
# is the publication event regardless of what any publish-path filter does. The
# parsers write here; the files never enter git. See NOTICE for the per-source
# terms and tests/test_licensed_text_not_tracked.py for the enforcement.
data/processed/frameworks/etsi.json
data/processed/frameworks/iso_27001.json

# A fold's predictions.json quotes the held-out framework's control text
# verbatim -- that is what a prediction record IS. When the held-out framework
# is a restricted source, that file is the licensed prose itself.
#
# Found 2026-08-28 the hard way: arm A1's ISO 27001 fold was committed and
# PUSHED carrying Annex A control statements verbatim -- 52 of its 93 rows
# match the fingerprint corpus, 30 of them on every window, longest contiguous
# run 35 words. The tree-wide fingerprint gate caught it, which is the gate
# working; the failure was that the gate ran after the push rather than before.
#
# This comment used to quote the offending sentence, which reproduced a 13-word
# run of Annex A in a file the gate cannot read: _SCANNED_SUFFIXES keys on
# Path.suffix and Path(".gitignore").suffix is "". Describing the breach is
# sufficient; quoting it re-commits it somewhere nothing checks.
#
# ISO 27001 is in the VALIDATION roster, so every validation arm reproduces
# this. One line per member of tract.config.OVERLAY_FRAMEWORK_IDS -- not the
# narrower RESTRICTED set, because DSOMM contributes about half the fingerprint
# corpus and had no line here until 2026-08-28. The fold directory is named
# from the link's standard_name (run_fold.py:313), which is why DSOMM's entry
# is the parenthesised display name and not the framework id.
# test_every_overlay_framework_has_a_fold_predictions_gitignore_line enforces
# this; before that test existed, this comment claimed an enforcement that did
# not exist.
# fold_result.json is unaffected: it carries indicators and metrics, not prose.
results/phase1b/**/fold_ISO_27001/predictions.json
results/phase1b/**/fold_ETSI/predictions.json
results/phase1b/**/fold_DevSecOps_Maturity_Model_(DSOMM)/predictions.json
# One line per member of tract.config.CONDITIONAL_FRAMEWORK_IDS, and there are
# two of them. Five CC BY-SA frameworks left this list once LICENSES/ shipped
# the real texts and NOTICE gained the modification statement: twelve of the
# corpus's thirteen copyleft sources are tracked, and holding five of them out
# while seven sat in git was a distinction no reading supports. The tier
# comment in tract/config.py carries the full reasoning.
#
# dsomm stays for a reason the five do not share: GPL-3.0's share-alike
# attaches to the whole work rather than to the adapted part. Its assignments
# stay tracked and published either way, because a mapping is a fact about two
# documents rather than a reproduction of either.
#
# csa_ccm.json LEFT this list on 2026-08-26, owner decision D1(b). It had to
# leave with the tier: OVERLAY_FRAMEWORK_IDS decides what merge_all_controls
# withholds from the tracked corpus, and this list decides what a fresh clone
# receives, and the two have to agree. They did not for one commit -- csa_ccm's
# prose was tracked inside all_controls.json while its per-framework JSON was
# still ignored, so CI had a framework the corpus described and the tree could
# not produce. tests/test_corpus_acceptance.py::TestSpecAcceptance caught it.
data/processed/frameworks/dsomm.json
data/processed/licensed/

# An overlay transfer archive carries ETSI, ISO 27001 and DSOMM prose.
# scripts/stage_licensed_overlay.py refuses to write one inside the tree, but a
# plain `tar czf` does not, and the whole point of these lines is that the
# escape happens through the channel nobody predicted. Belt and braces.
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.tar
# Repair audit records quote source text verbatim on both sides of a move, so
# a restricted framework's audit file carries the same licensed prose the two
# rules above keep out of git.
data/processed/repair_audit/
# `tract export-canonical` writes full control text for every framework it is
# given, and its default output directory sits at the repository root, so
# `tract export-canonical && git add -A` staged licensed prose. This line is
# the smaller half of the fix. The command's stated destination is an OpenCRE
# RFC, a third-party channel outside git that no .gitignore rule reaches, so
# tract/export/canonical.py withholds the text itself. See
# tests/test_canonical_export.py.
canonical_export/
# The same defect in the sibling path. `tract export --opencre` writes a
# `<Standard>|description` column holding the publisher's own control statement,
# and its default output directory is ./opencre_export at the repository root.
# This line stops the NEXT run's output from being staged. It does not untrack
# the seven files already in git: those were committed before the filter
# existed, `git rm` on them would move published metrics and un-publish
# nothing, and the framework they expose is an open owner question recorded in
# NOTICE. tract/export/opencre_csv.py withholds the text itself, which is the
# half of the fix that reaches OpenCRE's importer.
opencre_export/

# Claude-specific working documentation. Analyses, runbooks and summaries that
# support the work without being part of it. Untracked on purpose.
claudedocs/*
# One exception. The Jetson drives the RunPod fleet and gets this repository by
# cloning it, so a handoff written to an untracked directory is a handoff that
# never arrives. Tracked deliberately and by name, which keeps the rest of
# claudedocs/ the scratch space it was meant to be.
!claudedocs/jetson-runpod-start.md

# Phase 2C bridge corpora. Every record carries a named annotator and their
# free text, and the default output lands in a tracked directory. Publishing a
# volunteer's identity and verbatim words to a public repository is not a
# decision an import command should make.
data/training/hub_links_bridge*.jsonl
data/training/hub_links_bridge*.reviewed.json
